Namespaces are a feature that was introduced in C++ to allow you to have names that are not in the global namespace. They're used to avoid name collisions between different libraries. For example, if you have two libraries that both have a class called MyLibrary, then you can put them in different namespaces, and then you can use them both without having to rename one of them.

Namespaces are also used to organize your code. If you have a large project with many files, you can put them in different namespaces to make it easier to find the code you need.

To create a namespace, you use the namespace keyword followed by the name of the namespace. For example:

namespace MyLibrary {
// code here
}


You can also put code in a namespace without creating a new namespace. For example:

namespace {
// code here
}


This is called an unnamed namespace, and it's useful for organizing code that you don't want to put in a specific namespace.

You can use the using keyword to bring a name into the current scope. For example:

namespace MyLibrary {
void func() {}
}

using MyLibrary::func;

func(); // calls MyLibrary::func()


In this example, we're using the using keyword to bring the name func into the current scope. We can then call func() without having to specify the namespace.

